    "result": "I implemented `plan_extraction` in `solution.py` and wrote 45 tests in `tests/test_solution.py`. `python -m pytest -q tests` passes all 45. The implementation follows `spec.md`: both `/` and `\\` separate segments, `.` and empty segments are dropped, `..` pops a segment, and any climb above the destination raises `UnsafePathError`. It also rejects NUL characters, absolute paths, drive prefixes and members that normalise to nothing. Directory entries are validated but left out of the result. Duplicate file targets are detected case-insensitively. Any unsafe member raises before anything is returned, so no partial plan is produced. I could not run a coverage tool, since only the test command was allowed. By reading the code, every line of `solution.py` is exercised by at least one test, so I expect the 90% gate to pass.\n\nTwo choices the spec left open: a drive prefix is any ASCII letter followed by `:` (so `a:b` is rejected as a drive path), and a file and a directory entry with the same name (`a` and `a/`) are not treated as duplicates. Neither case is covered by the spec's rules, so tell me if you want either handled differently. I did not create or commit any other files.",
